<Project>
  <!--
    Flows into apps that consume the CupriFace.Web.NativeAot NUPKG.

    WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, AND WHY YOUR CSPROJ STILL HAS WORK IN IT. The ILCompiler.LLVM backend
    lives on the dotnet-experimental feed, not nuget.org, and a package cannot add a restore source
    to its consumer: restore has already chosen its sources by the time any of our build files are
    read. So the app must declare the feed, the ILC packages, and the two native-asset packages
    itself. The package notes carry the block to paste; samples/WebLlvm is a working copy of it.

    That is a real limitation of this host rather than an oversight, and it is one of the honest
    differences from CupriFace.Web.Mono, which needs none of it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- browser-wasm is the only RID this host runs on; setting it here means an app's csproj does
         not have to know that, and a wrong one fails at restore rather than at link. -->
    <RuntimeIdentifier Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''">browser-wasm</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <!-- NOT PublishAot. The SDK's own AOT switch rejects browser-wasm outright
         (NETSDK1203) — this host reaches ILC through the ILCompiler.LLVM packages the app
         references, with PublishTrimmed driving the trim, which is a different road to the
         same compiler. -->

    <!-- The engine and this host are browser-only by construction; an app that writes no interop
         should not inherit the platform-compatibility noise from them. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1416</NoWarn>

    <!-- WebGL2 available to anything the app links. The host itself does not use GL — it rasterises
         with Skia and presents through putImageData — but a surface underlay does, and this is the
         one setting an app cannot discover from its symptoms: without it
         emscripten_webgl_create_context SILENTLY DOWNGRADES a version-2 request to WebGL1 rather
         than refusing it, and the first sign is "ERROR: unsupported shader version" from a
         `#version 300 es` shader, which points at the shader rather than at the context.

         Additive, so an app can still raise it; MIN is deliberately NOT set, because a page that
         only ever rasterises has no reason to be refused a WebGL1 context it will never ask for.

         PRICED AND KEPT UNCONDITIONAL. Measured by relinking samples/WebLlvm without it: +9,447
         bytes of wasm and +56,745 of JS glue, so 66 KB uncompressed that an app which never touches
         WebGL still carries. Making it opt-in would hand that back, and was rejected: the failure it
         prevents is invisible at the point it happens. A consumer who has not read this file gets a
         shader error naming a line they did not write, in a context they cannot inspect, on a build
         that linked cleanly. 66 KB is a worse trade than one developer losing a day to that. If the
         payload ever matters more than the trap, the change is one condition here, not a redesign. -->
    <EmccFlags>$(EmccFlags) -sMAX_WEBGL_VERSION=2</EmccFlags>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
